# SSM Parameter Stacks

## Input shape

```hcl
inputs = {
  parameters = [
    {
      comp        = "ENV"               # COMP domain - uppercase
      app_alias   = upper(local.app_alias) # always uppercase here
      name        = "LOG_LEVEL"         # uppercase, snake-case
      value       = "INFO"              # placeholder for secrets
      type        = "String"            # or "SecureString" for secrets
      description = "Controls logging level for <app>"
    },
    # ...
  ]
}
```

## Path format

Resulting parameter path: `/{COMP}/{APP_ALIAS}/{NAME}` (all uppercase). See `naming-conventions.mdc` for `COMP` domains.

## Secrets

- Use `type = "SecureString"` for any sensitive value.
- Set `value = "WillBeChanged"` (or similar placeholder) in code.
- The `tf-ssm_param` module uses `lifecycle { ignore_changes = [value] }`, so the real value must be set out-of-band (Console or `aws ssm put-parameter`) and Terraform never sees or rotates it.
- Never commit real credentials to satisfy the `value` requirement.

## Required fields

Each parameter object must have `comp`, `app_alias`, `name`, `value`, `type`, `description`. Keep `description` informative — it appears in the AWS Console and helps app developers.

## Cross-app references

When an app needs another app's value, reference the existing path (e.g. `/MQ/NYX/MQ_ENDPOINT` published once in the landscape ssm_param stack, consumed by `mercury` and `perseus` ECS services). Do not duplicate the parameter under each app.

## Dependencies

Stacks producing the values referenced (e.g. DDB tables, S3 buckets, RabbitMQ broker) should be declared as `dependency` blocks so plans run in the right order.
